$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>How a DV case produced a prosecution of the complainant</h2>
<p>The sequence matters. The appellant&rsquo;s Section 12 DV complaint was dismissed in default on 9 October 2024. To have the dismissal undone on appeal, she filed a medical certificate dated the same day. The respondent objected; the Principal District &amp; Sessions Judge, Karkardooma, had the SHO verify it; the report said the clinic had functioned only in 2019-20, had since closed, and that the named doctor had never practised there or issued any certificate. On 23 December 2025 the Sessions Judge recorded the certificate as prima facie fake, whereupon the appellant withdrew her appeal. By order dated 17 March 2026 the Sessions Judge directed a complaint against her. It is that direction the High Court has now affirmed.</p>

<h2>The legal architecture</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>The offence.</strong> Fabricating false evidence for use in a judicial proceeding — Sections 192 and 193 IPC, now Sections 228 and 229 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The gateway.</strong> Offences under clause (b)(i) of Section 195(1) CrPC — now Section 215(1) BNSS — can be prosecuted only on the complaint of the court concerned, through the Section 379 BNSS procedure.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The filter.</strong> The court is not bound to complain merely because an offence appears committed; it must find prosecution expedient in the interests of justice (Iqbal Singh Marwah, para 23).</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The carve-out that did not apply.</strong> The custodia legis limitation — that forgery committed before a document enters court is outside clause (b)(ii) — was irrelevant, because fabricating false evidence falls under clause (b)(i).</div>
</div>

<h2>The appellant&rsquo;s arguments, answered</h2>
<p>Three points were pressed and rejected. The reference to Section 340 CrPC instead of Section 379 BNSS caused no prejudice: the procedure actually followed matched the governing provision. The Iqbal Singh Marwah custodia legis point failed because the charge is fabrication of false evidence, not forgery of a document later produced — a distinction the Supreme Court itself drew in para 10 of that decision. And the argument on Section 343(1) CrPC (Section 382(1) BNSS) concerned only how the Magistrate will try the complaint, not whether it could be made. The Court stressed the limits of this stage: <em>&ldquo;the Court is not bound to make a complaint merely because an offence appears to have been committed&rdquo;</em> — but where a fabricated document is used to move the machinery of restoration, expediency is made out.</p>

<h2>A caution for matrimonial litigation generally</h2>
<p>Matrimonial and DV files are dense with medical certificates — explaining absences, grounding interim applications, supporting injury allegations. This judgment is a reminder that every such document is verifiable through the SHO at the opposite party&rsquo;s instance, and that the consequence of a failed verification is not merely the loss of the application. It is a separate criminal complaint by the court itself, which no withdrawal of the underlying appeal can recall. Litigants on the receiving end of a suspect document have a defined remedy: a verification request, followed where warranted by an application invoking Section 379 BNSS.</p>
<div class="note">All findings at this stage are prima facie. Whether the certificate was fabricated, and with what knowledge and intention, will be decided by the Magistrate on evidence at trial. Case details are as recorded in the judgment dated 3 November 2026.</div>
